","title":"No Deleuze","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s2e8-no-deleuze/"},{"id":"s2e7-heights-depths-and-other-surfaces","title":"Heights, Depths, and Other Surfaces","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s2e7-heights-depths-and-other-surfaces/"},{"id":"a-response-to-bo-burnhams-inside","title":"A response to Bo Burnham's Inside","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/a-response-to-bo-burnhams-inside/"},{"id":"s2e6-amateur-philosophy-expertise-and-intimacy","title":"Amateur Philosophy, Expertise, and Intimacy","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s2e6-amateur-philosophy-expertise-and-intimacy/"},{"id":"s2e5-becoming-connotative","title":"Becoming Connotative","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s2e5-becoming-connotative/"},{"id":"s2e4-its-about-time","title":"It's About Time","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s2e4-its-about-time/"},{"id":"s2e3-the-structure-of-chance","title":"The Structure of Chance","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s2e3-the-structure-of-chance/"},{"id":"s2e2-parataxis-and-teaching","title":"Parataxis and Teaching","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s2e2-parataxis-and-teaching/"},{"id":"s2e1-starting-into-sense-plato-and-the-field-of-rhetoric","title":"Starting into Sense: Plato and the \"Field\" of Rhetoric","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s2e1-starting-into-sense-plato-and-the-field-of-rhetoric/"},{"id":"intro-to-deleuzecast","title":"Intro to Deleuzecast","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/intro-to-deleuzecast/"},{"id":"s1e12-the-essence-of-genius","title":"The Essence of Genius","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e12-the-essence-of-genius/"},{"id":"s1e11-the-language-of-theory","title":"The Language of Theory","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e11-the-language-of-theory/"},{"id":"s1e10-the-error-of-dread-why-ethics-isnt-for-us","title":"The Error of Dread: Why Ethics Isn't \"For\" Us","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e10-the-error-of-dread-why-ethics-isnt-for-us/"},{"id":"s1e9-propositions-and-other-delusions","title":"Propositions and Other Delusions","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e9-propositions-and-other-delusions/"},{"id":"s1e8-math-and-other-lifeless-repetitions","title":"Math and Other (Lifeless?) Repetitions","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e8-math-and-other-lifeless-repetitions/"},{"id":"s1e7-one-or-several-hegels","title":"One or Several Hegels?","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e7-one-or-several-hegels/"},{"id":"s1e6-cutting-blurring-and-the-agony-of-overcoming-the-human","title":"Cutting, Blurring, and the Agony of Overcoming the Human","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e6-cutting-blurring-and-the-agony-of-overcoming-the-human/"},{"id":"s1e5-on-the-curious-desire-for-conceptual-stability","title":"On the Curious Desire for Conceptual Stability","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e5-on-the-curious-desire-for-conceptual-stability/"},{"id":"s1e4-grading-and-other-essences","title":"Grading and Other Essences","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e4-grading-and-other-essences/"},{"id":"s1e3-in-distinction","title":"In/Distinction","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e3-in-distinction/"},{"id":"s1e2-how-to-read-theory-and-complications","title":"How to Read Theory (and Complications)","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e2-how-to-read-theory-and-complications/"},{"id":"introduction-to-the-podcast","title":"Introduction to the Podcast","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/introduction-to-the-podcast/"},{"id":"s1e1-styles-of-reading-academic-dispositions-hegels-appropriation-mac","title":"Styles of Reading / Academic Dispositions / Hegel's \"Appropriation Machine\"","url":"/thinking-with-staging/episodes/s1e1-styles-of-reading-academic-dispositions-hegels-appropriation-mac/"}],"writing":[{"id":"the-pleasure-of-being-persuaded","title":"The Pleasure of Being Persuaded","url":"/thinking-with-staging/writing/the-pleasure-of-being-persuaded/"},{"id":"owning-the-words","title":"Owning the Words","url":"/thinking-with-staging/writing/owning-the-words/"}],"provocations":[{"id":"2ae0e6c1ffca","date":"9/23/2026","text":"Let’s stop referring to being thoughtful about something as “taking it seriously.” It’s important not to equate attentive engagement with earnestness and solemnity!","url":"/thinking-with-staging/provocations/"},{"id":"c328ea192164","date":"9/22/2026","text":"freedom is not escaping the relations that constitute us, but acquiring some capacity to participate in the recomposition of those relations","url":"/thinking-with-staging/provocations/"},{"id":"2baf8d8586ca","date":"9/21/2026","text":"individuality is much harder to achieve than people think. people think it’s just a matter of standing out from “the crowd” in some way. but it has absolutely nothing to do with the crowd, but with your ability to calibrate how events impact you. the extent to which you want to be recognized as an individual is the extent to which you are not one.","url":"/thinking-with-staging/provocations/"},{"id":"32a27e907b2f","date":"9/20/2026","text":"Taking something for granted can also be a wonderful expression of generosity - to feel so comfortable and confident in another person’s behavior toward you that you take them for granted can be a deep compliment","url":"/thinking-with-staging/provocations/"},{"id":"7f2a1371d418","date":"9/14/2026","text":"Given that histories of wars have been to a large extent determined by technological superiority, why aren’t the nerds of the era the paradigm of subjectivity, and not the guy who gets in barfights?","url":"/thinking-with-staging/provocations/"},{"id":"70694b432284","date":"9/12/2026","text":"We aren’t beings who undergo training. We are ongoing effects of training that continue to remain trainable.","url":"/thinking-with-staging/provocations/"},{"id":"1ea0bb5f1a59","date":"9/9/2026","text":"criticism often figures the critic as a judge, whose job is to assign the proper value to an object. but that’s not a necessary role. Couldn’t the critic be figured as an interlocutor or a respondent or an apprentice or just a traveling passenger? Think of how dramatically different scholarly traditions would be if we assumed roles other than that of judges.","url":"/thinking-with-staging/provocations/"},{"id":"f15828448e38","date":"8/14/2026","text":"For Scholarship: Enough fidelity to the text and its traditions to encounter pushback; enough irreverence to make something different with it","url":"/thinking-with-staging/provocations/"}]}